{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-06-dividing-totals-in-ratios",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Dividing a Total in a Ratio",
    "description": "Turn ratio parts into actual shares, including inverse-share and group-distribution conditions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use a visible “parts” model from the first screen. Start with direct splitting, then inverse conditions and fractional relations, then group/per-person sharing and a nested capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach ratio shares as equal-sized units: add the ratio terms, find one unit, then multiply. Cover two-, three- and four-way division; inverse shares from equal multiples such as 3A=4B=7C; fraction relations such as A=3/2 B and B=7/4 C; conditions involving a share versus the combined shares of others; minimum whole-number distributions from fractional ratios; and group problems where different numbers of men, women and children receive different per-person shares. Include a nested split capstone."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio tells you how many equal parts each person or group receives.\n\nOnce the total number of parts is clear, large sharing questions become small mental divisions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "₹420 is shared in the ratio 4:3.\n\nThink of 7 equal boxes: 4 boxes for A, 3 for B.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "One box = 420 ÷ 7 = ₹60.\nA gets 4×60 = ₹240.\nB gets 3×60 = ₹180.\n\nRatio gives the shape; the total gives the size of one part.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
